./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:772:For `idea_03`, the horizon-tail condition is a clean **sufficiency** condition, but it is not a genuine **necessity** condition for instance-level learnability.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:774:The obstruction is not technical: large long-horizon tail reward can be perfectly predictable or distributionally known, so the learner may not need delayed reward feedback to choose the right quote. I found a concrete counterexample where `Delta_T(H_T)` is linear along infinitely many horizons for every `H_T log T = o(T)`, while a fixed learner has `o(T)` regret.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:778:The proposed replacement tail quantity is
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:808:**Lemma 1, sufficiency via horizon-tail error.**  
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:817:This is exactly the existing truncation/discretization argument with `T beta(H)` replaced by the ex ante comparator tail loss `Delta_T(H)`.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:826:Let spike times grow superexponentially, for example
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:856:The previous blocks contain only `S_{n-1}=o(S_n)` quotes total. Hence, even if the best fixed comparator extracts the maximum possible extra reward from all older blocks, its advantage over the always-`1/2` learner is at most `o(T)`. Between spike horizons, no new dominant block has resolved, so the same domination argument gives `o(T)` regret for all horizons.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:858:However, for `T=S_n` and any `H_T=o(T)`, the quotes in the last block with posting times at least `H_T` before `S_n` contribute to the tail error. Taking `a=1/2`,
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:868:So the horizon-tail condition fails strongly, even though sublinear regret is achievable.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:874:If the intended theorem is instead minimax or distribution-free over a class of processes unknown to the learner, then the counterexample is not decisive because the learner hard-codes the useful distributional fact that the one-spike optimizer is `1/2`.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:886:The counterexample is stronger than merely saying `Delta_T` takes a supremum over irrelevant actions. In the construction above, the linear tail is carried by the asymptotically optimal quote itself.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:888:Thus even replacing `sup_a` by a tail over near-optimal comparators would not fix necessity. The missing ingredient is informational: whether the long-delayed tail contains information the learner actually needs.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:890:A more plausible necessary condition would need to measure something like **unpredictable tail mass**, or be stated for classes closed under changing the unresolved tail law while preserving the learner’s observable history.
./logs/iter_001_idea_03_explorer_02.stderr.log:918:If the goal is instance-level characterization, `Delta_T(H)` is not necessary. If the goal is uniform learnability over a process class, try to formulate a lower bound using pairs of environments that agree on all observations available before delay `H` but have different optimal fixed quotes. That would test an informational tail condition rather than a raw reward-tail condition.
