./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:796:web search: online learning delayed feedback unbounded random delays regret tail condition
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:804:The payoff-weighted tail condition is a valid sufficient refinement:
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:811:It gives the same truncation proof with \(T\gamma(H)\) replacing \(T\beta(H)\). It is also exactly equivalent to uniform tail tightness on every positive action scale \(a\ge \epsilon\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:813:However, \(\gamma(h)\to0\) still looks too strong as a necessary condition. The essential supremum over histories and the supremum over times can fail because of rare or sparse exceptional histories whose total payoff contribution is \(o(T)\). A more natural condition for expected regret is a horizon-averaged payoff tail.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:874:The upper-bound proof survives the beta-to-gamma replacement without structural changes. Discretization is unchanged: rounding \(a\) down to a grid point loses at most \(T/K\). The finite-grid truncated problem has bounded delay \(H\), and because prices are revealed, counterfactual truncated rewards for all grid quotes become known after \(H\) steps.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:876:The necessity attempt for \(\gamma\) breaks: failure of an essential-supremum condition need not create linear expected regret. It only says that somewhere, after some history, a positive quote has a long conditional tail. It does not say that such histories occur often enough, or that late payoff mass is hard to predict.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:884:A true “only if” theorem needs a precise quantifier structure: instance-wise learnability, minimax over a process class, or uniform post-history learnability. Tail mass alone is not a hardness condition.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:888:Sparse deterministic obstruction to necessity of \(\gamma\): take epochs with \(n\) zeros followed by \(n^3\) ones. For any \(h\), choose \(n>h\) and the first zero in that block. For \(a=1/2\), the next trade occurs after more than \(h\) steps with probability \(1\), so \(\gamma(h)\ge 1/2\) for all \(h\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:890:But the total number of zero-block positions through epoch \(N\) is \(O(N^2)\), while total time is \(O(N^4)\). Hence the averaged tail mass is \(o(T)\), and the averaged truncation condition gives sublinear regret. This shows \(\sup_t\) is too strong for expected-regret necessity.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_02.stderr.log:914:Next, ask a counterexample-search worker to formalize the sparse-block construction and verify the regret bound under the averaged condition, including the max-versus-sup issue.
